# research.local.env.example
#
# Copy this file to `research.local.env` (same directory, the repository root) and edit it.
# `research.local.env` is git-ignored: it names machines on ONE person's network, which is exactly
# the thing this repository must not carry. This example is committed; the real file never is.
#
# THERE IS NOTHING TO DOWNLOAD. Every experiment here generates its own auxiliary-field
# configurations from a seed, so a fresh clone reproduces every number in the paper with no data
# release, no store and no configuration at all. Both settings below are OPTIONAL, and with the
# file absent entirely everything runs and builds on this machine. They exist because two jobs here
# are expensive enough that whoever has a machine for them should be able to say so in one place
# instead of in every caller:
#
#   * the test suite is about fourteen minutes, and seven experiments run past ten;
#   * the Lean development builds against Mathlib.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COMPUTE_* -- where heavy Python runs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `research/code/remote_run.py` holds the Python decision. A remote run ships the Python SOURCES
# and runs them there, so what runs is what is in this tree rather than whatever the remote
# checkout drifted to. It sets the import path at both ends, because everything here runs from
# `research/code` with that directory on PYTHONPATH.
#
#   COMPUTE_HOST=user@host                       # ssh destination; absent, runs are local
#   COMPUTE_PORT=22                              # optional
#   COMPUTE_KEY=~/.ssh/some_key                  # optional
#   COMPUTE_DIR=signproblem                      # remote working tree, relative to $HOME
#   COMPUTE_PY=$HOME/research-venv/bin/python    # the interpreter there, holding requirements.txt
#
#     python research/code/remote_run.py --describe     # where would a run go?
#     python research/code/remote_run.py --drift        # sources stranded on the remote
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEAN_BUILD_* -- where `lake build` runs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `research/code/lean_build.py` holds the Lean decision. A remote build ships the Lean SOURCES and
# compiles them there; it never ships or deletes `.lake`, which holds the Mathlib build the remote
# host exists for and is measured in gigabytes.
#
#   LEAN_BUILD_HOST=user@host            # ssh destination; absent, builds are local
#   LEAN_BUILD_PORT=22                   # optional
#   LEAN_BUILD_KEY=~/.ssh/some_key       # optional
#   LEAN_BUILD_DIR=signproblem-lean      # remote Lean checkout, relative to $HOME
#   LEAN_BUILD_PATH=$HOME/.elan/bin      # optional, prepended to the remote PATH
#
#     python research/code/lean_build.py                  # describe, then build
#     python research/code/lean_build.py --clear-dry-run  # what a cold elaboration would clear
#
# SEPARATE KEYS ON PURPOSE. The two roles may well be the same machine, but they are different
# questions -- a Lean builder wants a warm Mathlib, a compute host wants cores -- and reading one
# set of keys for both would make moving the reads move the build with them. There is no fallback
# from one to the other.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Precedence
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The environment variable of the same name is checked FIRST and wins over this file, so a single
# run can be pointed somewhere else, or forced local, without editing anything:
#
#     COMPUTE_HOST= python research/code/remote_run.py reads/expBD_route_calibration.py
